Lab 14: Manage File-Based Component Security with AIDE
Objectives
Install and configure AIDE (Advanced Intrusion Detection Environment) to monitor OpenStack configuration files
Schedule regular integrity checks using cron
Configure automated alerts for unauthorized file changes
Key Concepts: File integrity monitoring, security hardening, intrusion detection
Prerequisites
A Linux system (RHEL/CentOS 8/9 or Ubuntu 20.04/22.04) with:
Minimum 2GB RAM
20GB disk space
Root/sudo privileges
Basic OpenStack components installed (if testing on actual OpenStack environment)
Working internet connection for package installation
Task 1: Install and Configure AIDE
Subtask 1.1: Install AIDE Package
# For RHEL/CentOS:
sudo dnf install aide -y

# For Ubuntu:
sudo apt install aide -y
Verification:

aide -v
Expected Output: Should display AIDE version information

Troubleshooting:

If package not found, enable EPEL repository (RHEL/CentOS):
sudo dnf install epel-release -y
Subtask 1.2: Initialize AIDE Database
sudo aide --init
sudo mv /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new.gz /var/lib/aide/aide.db.gz
Explanation:

--init creates a new database of file checksums and attributes
The database is moved to the active location for future comparisons
Subtask 1.3: Configure AIDE for OpenStack Files
Edit the configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/aide.conf
Add these OpenStack-specific rules:

# OpenStack specific directories
/etc/nova       p+i+n+u+g+s+b+m+c+sha512
/etc/neutron    p+i+n+u+g+s+b+m+c+sha512
/etc/keystone   p+i+n+u+g+s+b+m+c+sha512
/etc/glance     p+i+n+u+g+s+b+m+c+sha512
/etc/cinder     p+i+n+u+g+s+b+m+c+sha512
Key Options:

p: permissions
i: inode
n: number of links
u: user
g: group
s: size
b: block count
m: mtime
c: ctime
sha512: checksum algorithm
Verification:

sudo aide --check
Expected Outcome: Initial check should show "AIDE found NO differences"

Task 2: Schedule Regular Integrity Checks
Subtask 2.1: Create Daily Check Script
sudo nano /usr/local/bin/aide_check.sh
Add content:

#!/bin/bash
OUTPUT=$(aide --check 2>&1)
if echo "$OUTPUT" | grep -q "AIDE found differences"; then
    echo "$OUTPUT" | mail -s "AIDE Alert: Unauthorized Changes Detected" admin@yourdomain.com
    logger -p auth.warn "AIDE: Unauthorized file changes detected"
fi
Make executable:

sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/aide_check.sh
Subtask 2.2: Schedule with Cron
sudo crontab -e
Add line:

0 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/aide_check.sh
Explanation: Runs daily at 2 AM

Verification:

sudo crontab -l
Task 3: Configure Automated Alerts
Subtask 3.1: Install Mail Utilities
# RHEL/CentOS:
sudo dnf install mailx postfix -y

# Ubuntu:
sudo apt install mailutils -y
Subtask 3.2: Test Alert System
Make a test change:
sudo touch /etc/nova/test_change
Run manual check:
sudo /usr/local/bin/aide_check.sh
Check mail queue:
mailq
Expected Outcome: You should receive an email alert about the change

Subtask 3.3: Update Database After Verified Changes
sudo aide --update
sudo mv /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new.gz /var/lib/aide/aide.db.gz
Conclusion
Summary of Achievements:

Successfully installed and configured AIDE for OpenStack security monitoring
Implemented scheduled integrity checks using cron
Established an automated alert system for unauthorized changes
Key Takeaways:

AIDE provides critical file integrity monitoring for security-sensitive environments
Regular checks help detect potential intrusions or misconfigurations
Automated alerts enable rapid response to security incidents
